## Attack, Detection, and Mitigation Summary

| Phase                | Attacker Activity              | Possible Detection                      | Recommended Mitigation             |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------ | --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Credential reuse     | Reused an exposed password     | Unusual authentication patterns         | Unique passwords and salted hashes |
| Horizontal movement  | Connected through WinRM        | Unexpected remote-management logins     | Restrict and monitor WinRM         |
| Privilege escalation | Abused group permissions       | Changes to privileged groups            | Audit delegation and group nesting |
| Data concealment     | Hid data inside an image       | Unusual file size or content            | DLP and content inspection         |
| Exfiltration         | Transferred data through SMB   | Unusual file access or destination      | Strong file and share permissions  |
| Covering tracks      | Removed tools and cleared logs | Log-clearing events and missing records | Centralized log forwarding         |

---

## Reflection

The biggest lesson I took away from this lab was that the same technical knowledge can be used from both the offensive and defensive sides of cybersecurity.

Every step I completed as an attacker created some type of evidence, whether it was a login event, a privileged-group change, a file transfer, or an attempt to clear logs. Understanding how the attack works makes it easier to understand what defenders should monitor.

This is the kind of experience I want to continue building: learning how systems are attacked while also learning how to detect, investigate, and prevent that activity.

